notes/codex_r10_frontier.md:42:**3. Deterministic subexponential factoring: what is actually missing?**
notes/codex_r10_frontier.md:48:| Dixon | **PROVED:** randomized \(L_N[1/2,O(1)]\). | A short, efficiently constructed deterministic candidate sequence guaranteed to produce useful smooth square relations. A density theorem over the entire residue space does not guarantee its first short deterministic segment succeeds. |
notes/codex_r10_frontier.md:49:| Original Schnorr–Lenstra random class groups | **CONJECTURED:** its analysis assumes smooth-class-number frequency. | Suitable discriminants with smooth class numbers, plus effective access to useful ambiguous classes. This is not an unconditional randomized benchmark. |
notes/codex_r10_frontier.md:50:| Lenstra–Pomerance | **PROVED:** randomized expected \(L_N[1/2,1]\). | Deterministic replacement for random class-group sampling and relation collection, guaranteeing enough useful relations. Small generators alone do not provide that guarantee. |
notes/codex_r10_frontier.md:52:Sources: [Dixon, §§2–3](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~cs812-1/dixon.pdf), [Schnorr–Lenstra, abstract](https://pub.math.leidenuniv.nl/~lenstrahw/PUBLICATIONS/1984c/artc.pdf), [Lenstra–Pomerance, §§1,5,8,10–11](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper85.pdf). LP §11 also identifies a serious flaw in the earlier random-class-group heuristic.
notes/codex_r10_frontier.md:56:> There exist fixed \(b,h>0\) such that, uniformly for every odd nonsquare composite \(N\), the \(B\)-smooth values
notes/codex_r10_frontier.md:63:This would give deterministic subexponential factoring: sieve the interval, compute a kernel basis, and test its dependencies. **A smooth-value count alone needs an additional useful-dependency guarantee.** Here “fixed polynomial” means fixed for each input; the theorem must be uniform in its \(N\)-dependent coefficients.
notes/codex_r10_frontier.md:65:Pomerance identifies the required smoothness estimates for \(x^2-N\) as essentially unresolved in the relevant ranges, and explicitly describes quadratic sieve as deterministic with conjectured complexity. [Pomerance, pp. 75,79](https://www.math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/qs08.pdf)
notes/codex_r10_frontier.md:67:**GRH:** I found no general deterministic subexponential integer-factoring bound from GRH alone. The earlier GRH class-group bound was still probabilistic; Shanks’s deterministic GRH bound is \(N^{1/5+o(1)}\). A uniform smooth-relations hypothesis would suffice, but it is additional to GRH. [Lenstra–Pomerance, introduction](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper85.pdf), [Harvey, introduction](https://arxiv.org/html/2010.05450#S1)
notes/codex_r10_frontier.md:100:**Falsifiable small experiment:** freeze \(b,h\) and a candidate schedule; test all eligible composites up to a manageable bound, then adversarial balanced semiprimes at larger sizes. Record smooth counts, kernel dimensions, and whether any kernel-basis dependency factors \(N\). A failing input falsifies the finite claimed schedule; success does not establish its asymptotic extension.
notes/codex_r10_frontier.md:102:**Adversarial assessment:** this is the longstanding quadratic-sieve/derandomization program, not a new algorithm. Novelty requires a restricted family for which one can actually prove the missing lemma. UW’s divisor covering does not supply a smooth-relation hitting theorem.

